\details{
Two estimation backends are provided:
\describe{
\item{\code{"gam"}}{Degenerate logistic regression on a case-1-control
design (Boschi, Lerner & Wit 2025): the response is a constant 1 and the
linear predictor is built from event-minus-control differences. Supports
smooth time-varying (\code{tv}), non-linear (\code{nl}) and time-varying
non-linear (\code{tvnl}) effects via \code{\link[mgcv:gam]{mgcv::gam()}}.}
\item{\code{"clogit"}}{Conditional logistic regression on a case-k-control
design via \code{\link[survival:clogit]{survival::clogit()}} (linear terms only). The case/control
strata are taken from \code{stratum}, or derived as \code{cumsum(case == 1)} when
\code{stratum} is \code{NULL} (assuming each case is immediately followed by its
controls, the eventnet blocked layout).}
\item{\code{"nn"}}{Flexible conditional-logistic models on the same
case-k-control design as \code{clogit}, trained by (mini-batch) gradient
descent on the exact risk-set softmax partial likelihood. Two
architectures via \code{\link[=nn_control]{nn_control()}}: a multilayer perceptron scoring the
full covariate vector jointly (interaction-capable), or an
\code{additive_spline} predictor — per-covariate B-spline expansions fitted
by stochastic gradient, the STREAM construction of Filippi-Mazzola &
Wit (2024, JRSS-C). No coefficient table; \code{summary()} reports
in-sample (and, with a validation split, held-out) concordance and
\code{plot(type = "pdp")} shows per-feature curves. Trained by the built-in
R engine, or by \pkg{torch} with \code{nn_control(engine = "torch")}.}
}
}
\section{Formula syntax}{

The right-hand side lists covariates. A bare name is a \strong{linear} effect; wrap
a name to request a smooth effect (\code{gam} method only):
\itemize{
\item \code{tv(x)}   — time-varying linear effect: \code{s(time, by = d_x)}.
\item \code{nl(x)}   — non-linear effect: \code{s(cbind(x_ev, x_nv), by = c(1, -1))}.
\item \code{tvnl(x)} — time-varying non-linear effect (tensor product).
\item \code{re(x)}    — random effect of a grouping factor \code{x} (e.g. the
sender), built from the matched \code{x_ev} / \code{x_nv} levels as
\code{s(cbind(x_ev, x_nv), by = cbind(1, -1), bs = "re")}, contributing
\code{f(event_level) - f(control_level)} (following the REM tutorial's
species-invasiveness term). Needs both \code{x_ev} and \code{x_nv} (as written
by \code{\link[=widen_case_control]{widen_case_control()}}); a single column \code{x} is an error, since it
gives the event and its control the same level and the term would be
zero. Identified only when the event and control differ on \code{x}.
}
Smooth terms take the basis settings of \code{\link[mgcv:s]{mgcv::s()}} / \code{\link[mgcv:te]{mgcv::te()}} as
named arguments after the covariate, so each term can use its own basis
dimension and type: \code{tv(x, k = 20, bs = "cr") + nl(y, k = 5, bs = "ps")}.
Any \code{s()} / \code{te()} argument other than \code{by} is accepted (e.g. \code{m}, \code{fx},
\code{sp}, \code{xt}); for \code{tvnl} a vector \code{k} or \code{bs} gives one value per margin,
time first (\code{tvnl(x, k = c(5, 8))}). Argument values are evaluated in the
formula's environment. A term without its own \code{k} / \code{bs} uses the global
\code{k} / \code{bs} arguments of \code{rem()}, and mgcv's defaults when those are \code{NULL}.
\code{re()} takes no further arguments (its basis is fixed to \code{bs = "re"}).

For the \code{gam} method the left-hand side is ignored (the response is the
constant case indicator); for \code{clogit} the left-hand side names the 0/1 event
indicator column (e.g. \code{event ~ x}), unless \code{case} is given explicitly.
The event indicator may be numeric or integer 0/1, logical, or a character
or factor column with values \code{"0"}/\code{"1"} or \code{"FALSE"}/\code{"TRUE"}; it is read
by value, and any other value is an error.

\code{offset()} terms enter the linear predictor with coefficient 1. For
\code{clogit} the expression is evaluated on the rows of \code{data}, as in
\code{\link[survival:clogit]{survival::clogit()}}. For \code{gam} it must name a column, \code{offset(o)}, which is
resolved like a linear term (an event-minus-control difference: column \code{o},
else \code{d_o}, else \code{o_ev - o_nv}). The \code{nn} method does not take offsets.
}

\section{Column resolution}{

For a covariate \code{x}, the event/control difference is taken from column \code{x},
else \code{d_x}, else \code{x_ev - x_nv}. Non-linear terms use \code{transform_x_ev} /
\code{transform_x_nv} when present (the eventnet spline-transformed covariate),
otherwise \code{x_ev} / \code{x_nv}. \code{tvnl} uses \code{transformed_time} when present.
Undirected logs (senders only, no receiver/\code{TARGET} column) are supported.
}

\section{Name clashes with other relational-event packages}{

The name \code{rem()} is used by three CRAN packages: this one,
\pkg{relevent} (on CRAN since 2010) and \pkg{redeem} (2026). Attaching
more than one of them masks the others, and R will say so at load time.

Fits from \code{amorem} have class \code{"amorem_rem"}, which the other two packages
do not use, so their S3 methods for class \code{"rem"} never apply to them. When
more than one of these packages is attached, call \code{amorem::rem()}
explicitly.
}

